    28	an empty set and `enabled` defaults to true. A registry with no pinned key is
    29	not trusted and produces a warning. Registry record cache TTL defaults to 3600
    30	seconds, offline grace to 604800 seconds, snapshot maximum age to 604800
    31	seconds, and future clock skew to 300 seconds. Zero cache TTL disables fresh
    32	cache hits; zero offline grace disables stale fallback; zero clock skew permits
    33	no future offset. The backend request limit defaults to 1048576 bytes and MUST
    34	NOT exceed 10485760 bytes.
    35	
    36	An OPTIONAL system configuration conforms to `system-config-v1.schema.json`
    37	and is merged before parsing the effective user configuration:
    38	
    39	1. `locked` contains only `audit_registries`,
    40	   `disable_builtin_registries`, `allowed_sources`, and `audit`;
    41	2. a locked key MUST be set by the system file and overrides a user value with
    42	   a warning naming the system file;
    43	3. an unlocked system key is a default and a user value wins;
    44	4. malformed or unreadable enforced configuration fails closed.
    45	
    46	Configuration writes MUST use a same-directory temporary file followed by
    47	atomic replacement. Implementations SHOULD serialize concurrent writers with a
    48	per-config lock and MUST never expose a partially written JSON object.
    49	
    50	## 2. Installation lifecycle
    51	
    52	For each project, the manager performs these phases in order:
    53	
    54	1. load `Skillfile.json`, or skip an absent manifest;
    55	2. determine effective agents and locale;
    56	3. verify generated project and adapter paths are ignored by git;
    57	4. load development substitutions and reject them under strict audit;
    58	5. add applicable hybrid declarations;
    59	6. build the dependency closure, including allowlist and snapshot checks;
    60	7. validate every skill package and manifest;
    61	8. reject active command collisions;
    62	9. verify system and legacy command dependencies;
    63	10. verify MCP requirements and emit static availability warnings;
    64	11. run source-audit policy;
    65	12. resolve trusted audit registries and reject revocation or strict unknown;
    66	13. detect moved tags;
    67	14. stop before persistent mutation for a dry run;
    68	15. materialize runtime, context, markers, environment files, and adapters;
    69	16. remove stale managed skills and shims;
    70	17. record the consumer and collect unreferenced runtime/cache entries.
    71	
    72	A failure before phase 15 MUST leave the existing installation unchanged. A
    73	dry run MUST NOT fetch into or otherwise mutate an existing source checkout,
    74	create a persistent source checkout, write a persistent snapshot or response
    75	cache, update audit or registry state, rewrite configuration, or create runtime
    76	or project installation artifacts. It MAY use network reads and temporary
    77	workspaces that are removed before the operation returns. A failure during
    78	materialization MUST roll the affected target back to its previous complete
    79	state. A manager MUST serialize concurrent installation of the same project.
    80	It MAY install independent projects concurrently.
    81	
    82	An upgrade resolves and fetches only the selected scope's direct declarations
    83	and their transitive dependency closure. An operation over several projects
    84	MUST deduplicate fetches of the same checkout. A global-scope upgrade fetches
    85	only the global dependency closure. A separate update operation MAY fetch all
    86	managed source repositories.
    87	
    88	The managed `.gitignore` comment is implementation-specific. Conformance
    89	depends on the generated paths being ignored, not on comment spelling.
    90	
    91	## 3. Runtime and project environment
    92	
    93	Runtime files are machine-local and keyed by skill name and resolved commit.
    94	Context installs under `.agents/skills/<name>/`; command shims install under
    95	`.agents/bin/`. Runtime-only nodes receive a marker-only directory and are not
    96	mirrored into agent adapters.
    97	
    98	Runtime roots are copied atomically. An existing commit-keyed entry MAY be
    99	reused only after verifying that every required path exists. Command launchers
   100	MUST be self-contained: executing a portable direct command location MUST NOT
   101	depend on shell activation or a user profile. A launcher prepends the project
   102	or global command directory, directories containing resolved declared system
   103	dependencies, and any implementation runtime directories required by the
   104	command. It preserves the inherited `PATH`, forwards arguments without
   105	reinterpretation, and returns the child command's exit status.
   106	
   107	On Unix a launcher is either a relative symlink when no environment
   108	augmentation is required or an executable POSIX-shell wrapper. On Windows it
   109	is a `.cmd` wrapper that safely quotes the runtime path, disables delayed
   110	expansion while constructing its environment, forwards all arguments, and
   111	returns the child status. Stale launchers owned by the previous plan are
   112	removed.
   113	
