   115	the portable `CSK_PROJECT_ROOT` to the resolved project root. They MUST locate
   116	themselves rather than rely on the caller's current directory. A manager MAY
   117	also set tool-specific variables.
   118	
   119	Project command availability for agents MUST NOT depend on a user shell
   120	profile. The portable direct command locations are
   121	`<project>/.agents/bin/<command>` on Unix and
   122	`<project>\.agents\bin\<command>.cmd` on Windows. A manager SHOULD warn when
   123	prompt-visible skill instructions infer an installed command from its source
   124	runtime path or omit shell-neutral shim resolution.
   125	
   126	A manager SHOULD publish forwarding shims for global commands into a safe,
   127	writable directory that is already on the user `PATH`. It MUST NOT overwrite
   128	an unmanaged entry. When no safe directory exists, it retains the canonical
   129	global shims and warns with the canonical location and optional shell
   130	activation path. Forwarding locations and their ownership records are
   131	machine-local and implementation-specific.
   132	
   133	## 4. Install scopes
   134	
   135	### 4.1 Project
   136	
   137	Project declarations materialize inside the project checkout. Different
   138	projects may install different commits independently.
   139	
   140	### 4.2 Global
   141	
   142	Global declarations use a machine-local `Skillfile.json`, context store,
   143	runtime shims, and environment files. Their physical location is
   144	implementation-specific. Global adapters mirror to each selected agent's home
   145	discovery directory. Global command precedence is below project shims and above
   146	the pre-activation system `PATH`.
   147	
   148	### 4.3 Hybrid
   149	
   150	A hybrid manifest extends Skillfile schema 1 with REQUIRED per-skill `targets`.
   151	A target is a project alias, exact resolved path, or path glob. The glob syntax
   152	is `/`-separated and implements only `*`, `?`, and `**`; matching is
   153	case-sensitive and does not depend on the host OS.
   154	
   155	Applicable hybrid declarations join the project closure. Precedence is
   156	project, hybrid, global. Project declarations shadow hybrid declarations with a
   157	message. Hybrid-only closure nodes render once in a machine store with the
   158	machine locale and are mirrored into targeted project adapters without writing
   159	skill context into the project checkout.
   160	
   161	## 5. Agent adapters
   162	
   163	The standard agent identifiers and discovery surfaces are:
   164	
   165	| Agent | Project surface | Global surface |
   166	|---|---|---|
   167	| `claude_code` | `.claude/skills` | `~/.claude/skills` |
   168	| `codex_cli` | `.codex/skills` | `~/.codex/skills` |
   169	| `cursor` | `.cursor/rules` | `~/.cursor/rules` |
   170	| `gemini` | `.gemini/skills` | `~/.gemini/skills` |
   171	| `opencode` | native `.agents/skills` | `~/.agents/skills` |
   172	| `windsurf` | native `.agents/skills` | `~/.agents/skills` |
   173	
   174	Adapter entries are symlinks, copies, or automatic symlink-with-copy-fallback.
   175	Every root carries the adapter ledger from Protocol Core section 10. Refresh is
   176	atomic per entry. Unknown agent identifiers produce a warning and no output.
   177	
   178	## 6. MCP requirements
   179	
   180	MCP verification is read-only. A manager MUST NOT launch, install, enable, or
   181	modify an MCP server while checking a skill requirement.
   182	
   183	| Agent | Project surface | User surface |
   184	|---|---|---|
   185	| `claude_code` | `.mcp.json` | `~/.claude.json` |
   186	| `cursor` | `.cursor/mcp.json` | `~/.cursor/mcp.json` |
   187	| `codex_cli` | `.codex/config.toml` | `~/.codex/config.toml` |
   188	| `gemini` | `.gemini/settings.json` | `~/.gemini/settings.json` |
   189	| `opencode` | `opencode.json`, `opencode.jsonc` | `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json(c)` |
   190	| `windsurf` | none | `~/.codeium/windsurf/mcp_config.json` |
   191	
   192	JSON surfaces use `mcpServers`, Codex TOML uses `mcp_servers`, and OpenCode uses
   193	`mcp`. A disabled entry does not count. Missing or malformed files configure no
   194	servers and produce a warning naming the file.
   195	
   196	`required_in: any` succeeds when at least one target agent configures the
   197	server. `all` requires every target. Failures name missing agents and include
   198	the declared hint. Markers record sorted agents where each server was found.
   199	
   200	A configured stdio server whose command is absent from `PATH` produces a
   201	warning when every discovered entry is positively stdio. Project-only server
   202	configuration produces a checkout-trust warning.
   203	
   204	## 7. Source audit policy
   205	
   206	Source audit is a machine-local policy layer. Detectors and analysis backends
   207	may differ; decisions do not.
   208	
   209	For each snapshot a manager computes the raw-tree content hash, runs a static
   210	canary, runs deterministic detectors, optionally invokes a configured backend,
   211	and records findings. A canary failure always blocks. Cloud backends receive
   212	only sources classified public by explicit policy; other egress attempts block.
   213	Secret redaction is REQUIRED before permitted cloud egress.
   214	
   215	Decisions are `allow`, `warn`, `block`, or `require_pin`:
   216	
   217	- local hash or source revocation always blocks;
   218	- strict mode requires an operator pin for pre-capability schemas;
   219	- a verifiable finding at or above `fail_on` blocks in strict mode;
   220	- advisory mode warns;
   221	- backend failure blocks in strict mode and warns in advisory mode;
   222	- `block` and `require_pin` fail installation.
   223	
   224	Pins record content hash, operator identity, reason, and creation time. Pins do
   225	not override local or registry revocation.
   226	
   227	## 8. Shell activation
   228	
   229	Shell activation is an OPTIONAL convenience for interactive users. A manager
   230	MUST NOT require it for agent command execution and MUST NOT modify a user
   231	shell profile without explicit user action.
   232	
   233	Shell activation searches upward from the current directory for the nearest
   234	`.agents/env.sh` or `.agents/env.ps1`. Entering or switching projects restores
   235	the saved pre-project `PATH` before sourcing the new environment. Leaving all
   236	projects restores that `PATH` and clears activation state. Nested projects use
   237	the nearest environment.
   238	
   239	Upward search MUST guarantee progress toward a filesystem root. A POSIX hook
   240	MUST treat an empty or non-absolute `PWD` as no active project. Before sourcing

